---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/seo/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { siteOrigin } from "../../config/site";
import { placePath, places } from "../../lib/seo/places";
import { gapSentence, newYorkEveningInTokyo, SAMPLE_DATES } from "../../lib/seo/zoneSamples";

const title = "Time Difference Between Cities";
const description =
  "Compare up to six cities on one world clock. See how daylight saving changes the hour gap, and when the date has already changed in the later city.";
const canonical = new URL("/time-difference/", siteOrigin).toString();

const londonNewYork = SAMPLE_DATES.map((date) => ({
  label: date.label,
  sentence: gapSentence("New York", "London", "Europe/London", "America/New_York", date.ms),
}));
const newYorkTokyo = SAMPLE_DATES.map((date) => ({
  label: date.label,
  sentence: gapSentence("Tokyo", "New York", "America/New_York", "Asia/Tokyo", date.ms),
}));
const evening = newYorkEveningInTokyo();

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How do I see the time difference between two cities?",
    answer:
      "Search each city on the world clock and add it to Compare cities. The tray holds up to six cities in this browser. The clocks stay on one shared instant, so the gap includes daylight saving when that zone is observing it.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why does the hour gap change for a few weeks?",
    answer:
      "Places that observe daylight saving do not all change on the same weekend. In March 2026 the United States has already moved clocks before the United Kingdom does, so London and New York are a different number of hours apart than they are in January or July. The sample list on this page shows those dates.",
  },
  {
    question: "What if it is already the next day in one city?",
    answer:
      "A large eastward gap crosses midnight. On 15 January 2026, 22:00 in New York is already the next calendar date in Tokyo. Compare cities shows both clocks for the same instant, including that date change.",
  },
];

const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "WebPage",
      name: title,
      description,
      url: canonical,
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: faqs.map((item) => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        name: item.question,
        acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.answer },
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} structuredData={structuredData}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Time difference", href: "/time-difference/" }]} />
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-paper-100">Time difference between cities</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-paper-400">
      TimeAtlas compares cities on one shared instant. It does not publish a separate page for every
      pair of cities. Those pages would repeat the same clock with the names swapped.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Compare cities</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        On the <a href="/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">world clock</a>, search a city, then
        add it under Compare cities. You can save up to six cities. The list stays in this browser
        only. It is not a public address, and adding a city does not create a new page for search
        engines. The same tray is on
        <a href="/clocks/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">world clocks by country</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Each clock uses the IANA time zone for that place and your device's time. The offset is the
        one in effect at that instant, including daylight saving. It is not a fixed number of hours
        stored for the city.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Pick a date without guessing the offset</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Time explorer, on the same page, sets a date and a time of day in UTC. Every saved clock then
        shows that one instant. UTC is used on purpose: during a daylight-saving change, a local time
        such as 1:30 can happen twice or not at all. Choosing the instant in UTC avoids that
        ambiguity. Return to now puts the clocks back on your device's current time.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        The explorer does not put the date into the web address. A shared meeting plan is not a
        separate indexable URL.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">When daylight saving changes the gap</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        London and New York both observe daylight saving, but not on the same weekend. In 2026 the
        United States moves in early March and the United Kingdom moves at the end of March. Mid-January
        and mid-July are not those transition weeks. Mid-March is.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-paper-300">
        {londonNewYork.map((row) => <li>{`On ${row.label}, ${row.sentence}.`}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-paper-300">
        The hours come from Europe/London and America/New_York at 12:00 UTC on each date. Search both
        cities and set Time explorer to the week you are planning if the meeting falls near a clock
        change.
        <a href="/time/london/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">Time in London, England</a>
        and
        <a href="/time/new-york/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">time in New York City</a>
        explain each zone.
        <a href="/daylight-saving/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">Which countries don't observe daylight saving</a>
        lists places that never make this shift.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">When the date changes</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Tokyo does not observe daylight saving, so its gap with New York changes only when New York does.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-paper-300">
        {newYorkTokyo.map((row) => <li>{`On ${row.label}, ${row.sentence}.`}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-paper-300">
        {
          `Date rollover is separate from that hour count. At ${evening.newYork} in New York, it is ${evening.tokyo} in Tokyo. ${
            evening.nextCalendarDate
              ? "Those are different calendar dates."
              : "Those fall on the same calendar date."
          } That example is 22:00 in America/New_York on 15 January 2026, not the live time. `
        }
        <a href="/time/tokyo/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">Time in Tokyo</a>
        {" uses Asia/Tokyo."}
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">City pages that are actually different</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        A handful of cities have their own page because the rule is different: a half-hour offset, no
        daylight saving, a southern-hemisphere summer, or a name that belongs to more than one place.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-paper-300">
        {
          places.map((place) => (
            <li>
              <a href={placePath(place)} class="underline hover:text-paper-100">
                Time in {place.regionLabel}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Questions</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 space-y-5">
        {
          faqs.map((item) => (
            <div>
              <dt class="font-semibold text-paper-100">{item.question}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1 text-paper-300">{item.answer}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
